/T (The sands of the Sinai are shifting as Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser nationalizes the Suez Canal, challenging decades of European influence in the Middle East. Britain and France see the move as a threat to their economic and geopolitical interests, while the United States, Soviet Union, and United Nations weigh their own responses. As delegates in an emergency session of the United Nations Security Council, you must navigate competing claims of sovereignty, international law, global commerce, and military escalation. The future of the postwar world hangs in the balance.)
